Instruction: Final report (all numbers come from the camera/instrument records; no analysis script was run).

Setup: Hikrobot MV-CS028-10UM, 12-bit (max 4095 DN), gain 0 dB, central ROI 400x400 at (768,532). Lab limit was 16 frames per capture, so each exposure used 16 frames, not the 200 in the plan. Lens capping and dark frames were not done. The room lights are a white surface under mains lighting.

Exposure scan (16-frame mean, max pixel, saturated pixels):
- 1000 us: 301.09 DN (0.079 FS), max 331, 0 saturated
- 2000 us: 540.24 DN, max 583, 0 saturated
- 5000 us: 1251.61 DN (0.318 FS), max 1317, 0 saturated
- 8333 us: 2041.56 DN (0.515 FS), max 2130, 0 saturated
- 10000 us: 2436.85 DN (0.613 FS), max 2534, 0 saturated
- 12000 us: 2902.14 DN (0.728 FS), max 3015, 0 saturated
- 16667 us: 3994.7 DN, max 4093 (p99.9 = 0.9995 FS), 0 saturated pixels but no headroom, so rejected.

Chosen exposure: 10 ms (10000 us), gain 0 dB. It is one full period of 100 Hz mains flicker, so the intensity ripple averages out. It is well exposed, with a mean of about 0.59-0.61 FS and a max of 2497-2534 DN (about 0.61 FS). It has 0 saturated pixels. It is also the shortest exposure that meets the well-exposed level with margin.

Flicker strength, from the frame-mean time series (16 frames at 0.137 s interval, which is not commensurate with 100/120 Hz):
- Short exposure, 1 ms: mean 296.892 DN, relative std 0.1411 %, min/max 296.18/298.06 DN. This frame is far too dark to count as well exposed.
- Chosen exposure, 10 ms: mean 2390.202 DN, relative std 0.0494 %, min/max 2388.33/2392.06 DN. The first-to-last drift is 0.0084 %.
- Flicker is about 2.9 times lower at 10 ms than at 1 ms (0.1411 / 0.0494).

Caveats:
- The flicker is weak in absolute terms. Part of the 1 ms relative std is shot and read noise rather than flicker, and I did not separate the two.
- Only 16 frames per exposure were taken, so the relative std is uncertain by roughly 20 %.
- The 10 ms choice is backed by the series comparison and the single-exposure scan, not by a fitted flicker frequency or a full scan of CV versus exposure. For the scan frames only the per-capture temporal noise was reported (for example 19.83 DN rms at 10 ms).
Reason: A verified measurement exists, with frame-mean series at both the short and chosen exposures, so the run can end.

